@model ApplicationEditorViewModel
@* Section 1. The same partial renders editable or read-only (IsReadOnly is set by the server). *@
@*
    Section 1, Applicant Information (4.a.i). The model is the page's ApplicationEditorViewModel (4.b), so this gets
    used on its own for section 1 and again inside _Summary.

    Read-only (4.d): a single <fieldset disabled> locks every input at once, so there's no if/else on each field and
    the markup is the same either way. IsReadOnly is true when you can't edit (you're a manager, or it's not Draft or
    Returned) and always on the Summary. Disabled inputs don't get posted, and the server rejects edits to locked
    applications anyway - this is just how it looks, not the actual security.

    Validation: asp-for on "ApplicantInformation.X" gives the fields their prefixed names plus the data-val-* attributes
    from the DataAnnotations, so errors show in the browser as you type. The section saves even with errors; after
    that, the controller puts the saved section's errors in ModelState under the same names, so asp-validation-for
    shows each one under its field - here and on the Summary - until it's fixed.
*@
<h4>Applicant information</h4>
<fieldset disabled="@Model.IsReadOnly">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-md-6 mb-3">
            <label asp-for="ApplicantInformation.Name" class="form-label"></label>
            <input asp-for="ApplicantInformation.Name" class="form-control" autocomplete="name" />
            <span asp-validation-for="ApplicantInformation.Name" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 mb-3">
            <label asp-for="ApplicantInformation.Phone" class="form-label"></label>
            <input asp-for="ApplicantInformation.Phone" class="form-control" autocomplete="tel" />
            <span asp-validation-for="ApplicantInformation.Phone" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 mb-3">
            <label asp-for="ApplicantInformation.Email" class="form-label"></label>
            <input asp-for="ApplicantInformation.Email" class="form-control" autocomplete="email" />
            <span asp-validation-for="ApplicantInformation.Email" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 mb-3">
            <label asp-for="ApplicantInformation.CurrentAddress" class="form-label"></label>
            <input asp-for="ApplicantInformation.CurrentAddress" class="form-control" autocomplete="street-address" />
            <span asp-validation-for="ApplicantInformation.CurrentAddress" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>
    </div>
</fieldset>
